WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.160
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.400
This piece of paper looks about as white as white can get.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.960
But what if I told you the color white doesn’t exist?

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.120
Do you really expect me to believe that?

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.920
Not without an explanation.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.240
Obviously.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.340
OK, so the reason we see anything at all is because of light, so let’s start there.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.180
The word “light” applies to anything on the EM spectrum.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.200
These category names are adjectives:

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.640
Radio light, infrared light, ultraviolet light.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.760
The universe is full of light.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.000
It’s just that we can’t see most of it.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.480
The only type of light we can see is this little tiny sliver right here,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.560
which we call visible light for obvious reasons.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.500
We see almost none of the light in the universe.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.420
But we do see enough to, you know, function on a daily basis.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.540
I mean, the vast majority of the light emitted by the Sun is visible,

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:04.760
so it shouldn’t be surprising that animals evolved to see light in or near this range.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.920
Seeing in x-rays or radio just wouldn’t be that useful.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.140
Anyway, specific colors are kind of like sub-categories.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.720
If we zoom in on the visible part of the spectrum,

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.100
we can see how the frequency or wavelength of light corresponds to color.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.560
Do you see any white in here? I don't.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.060
Check! Mate!

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.500
White does not exist!

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:25.660
Wait a minute!

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.720
How the heck is your lab coat white then?

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.480
OK, I suppose I could elaborate.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.560
Sure, we assign colors to each of these wavelengths,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.540
but does that mean these wavelengths are those colors?

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:41.500
There are plenty of colors we can see that don’t correspond to a single wavelength.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.960
Black, white, and magenta are three big examples.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.620
So clearly there’s more to color than just light.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.020
Hmm.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.480
Well, we know light is necessary.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.920
Without it, all we see is black.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.920
Black is the absence of light.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.240
So, we definitely need a source of light like the Sun or a bulb or something.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.760
Then we need the thing we’re trying to see, and then our eye balls, and then our brains.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.440
This is getting overwhelming. Let's look at this as one big process.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.720
Lots of light is emitted by the Sun.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.520
Some of that light reflects off of this leaf.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.680
Even less of that light actually enters your eyes,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.820
which then send signals to your brain,

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.440
which, in turn, has to interpret those signals.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.120
Let’s start with that reflection.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.600
Remember, the Sun emits all the colors in the visible spectrum.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.480
The reason this leaf looks green is because that’s the color the leaf reflects.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.560
Let me repeat that for everyone in the back.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.700
Objects do not have color.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.040
Light has color.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.680
Objects just absorb some colors and reflect others.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.760
I’m totally serious. My phone case isn’t red.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.620
It just reflects red light.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.960
If you put it under different colored light, it’ll be a different color.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.640
If something looks white, that just means it reflects all the colors,

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.319
but that can also happen with things that are transparent.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.500
Take a nice piece of plastic for example.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.980
You might be inclined to say “It doesn’t have color. It's clear."

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.020
But, if you fold it enough times, you’ll notice something weird.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.620
It’ll start to turn white.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.120
This happens because transparent materials aren’t perfect.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.069
There’s always a little reflection.

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:14.580
If there’s only one layer of plastic, the reflection is insignificant.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.420
But, every fold adds extra reflections.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.240
Eventually, most of the light gets reflected instead of passing through.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.620
The same thing happens with polar bear fur.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.400
It’s also transparent.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.780
It’s just that the fur is layered enough with air to maximize reflections.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.159
You can turn the reflection back down a bit

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.740
by filling the air spaces with something else, like water,

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.660
which is exactly how the wet t-shirt effect works.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.060
The point is: Objects don’t have color. Light does.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.280
The next step in the process is how our eyes actually respond to the light.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.240
It’s time for a crash course in human eye biology.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:54.200
The inside back of the eye is covered in cells called photoreceptor cells,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.780
which literally means “light receiver.”

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.680
It’s kind of nice when a scientific name actually makes sense.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.420
Those photoreceptor cells come in two types: rods and cones,

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:07.180
which is literally a description of their shape.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.060
The rods only detect the intensity of light, so they're not really help with color vision.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.200
The cones are where the magic happens.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.680
At the top of each cone is a stack of membranous

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.940
containing tiny collections of a protein called iodopsin

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.500
This stimulates another protein called transducin

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.370
This enzyme degrades a secondary messenger chemical

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:25.980
in the cone's cytoplasm

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.280
The decrease in cGMP closes

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.200
Thus hyper-polarizing

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.240
We call this a cyclic

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.480
Huh? What? What? What? What?

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.500
OK, here’s what actually matters.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:38.740
Light hits the cones and a chemical process sends a signal to the brain.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.100
That signal contains information about color

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.720
because there are three different types of cones: red, green, and blue.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.400
They’re not actually that color.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.220
Most things in the human body are roughly the same pinkish-brown color.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.480
These types of cones are called red, green, and blue cones

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.280
because that’s the type of light they each respond to.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.300
Wait wait, our eyes can only see red, green, and blue?

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.440
Yeah, pretty much.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.120
Then how does our brain know about the other colors?

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.480
Mixed signals!

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.720
This is one of those rare cases when mixed signals are a good thing.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.780
If you graph the sensitivity of cone cells against the visible spectrum,

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.480
you get something like this.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.740
Now let’s say orange light enters your eye.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:22.140
That light will activate the red cones and green cones by a very specific amount,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.060
which sends a unique mixed-signal to the brain.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.180
No other color creates that signal combination.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:31.400
The same is true for every other color on the visible spectrum.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:32.800
But here’s the thing:

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.440
There are signal combinations the brain can receive that aren’t created by any single wavelength of light.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:42.340
What if the blue and red cones activate intensely at the same time?

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.180
None of the colors on the visible spectrum are going to do that,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.840
but, if your brain gets that signal, it’s got to make sense of it somehow.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.720
So it makes it look like this: magenta.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.360
Your brain literally made up a color for a hypothetical signal combination

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.440
and that’s not the only color it made up!

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.440
What if all three types of cones activate intensely at the same time?

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.780
Well, that’s what your brain calls white.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:09.600
In fact, if you total up all the possible signal combinations the brain can receive,

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.920
you get about 10 million different colors.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.260
Colors we can summarize with cool animations like this.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.420
Actually, this reminds me of another point I should probably make.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.600
This isn’t actually yellow.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:25.600
See, ten million different colors is a lot to engineer into an LCD screen

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.820
like the one you’re using to watch this video.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.860
So we’ve designed screens to play tricks on your brain.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:33.600
Your screen is made of a bunch of pixels.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.800
If you make those pixels small enough you get the illusion of a continuous image,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:39.680
but what if you want a color image?

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.020
Your eyes can only see three types of colors: red, green, and blue;

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.360
so that’s all your screen has to display.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:50.760
Red and green set to full brightness is going to look like yellow to your brain,

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.380
even if it isn’t yellow.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.640
If each color in a pixel has 256 different brightness levels,

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:03.680
the screen has a total of 16,777,216‬ different colors it can display,

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.380
which is well over the 10 million or so colors we can see.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:12.160
The point is we already can’t see a bunch of the colors that your screens are displaying.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.900
Any additional colors marketed to you are a complete waste of your money.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.600
So is white a real color?

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.640
Not really.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.420
You won’t find the color white on the visible spectrum

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.140
like you would most other colors.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:26.280
It’s not a color connected to a single wavelength of light.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.540
But if something reflects enough different colors at the same time,

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:33.300
all three different types of cone cells will activate at the back of your eye

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.580
and send a strong signal to your brain.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.640
Your brain interprets that signal as white.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:43.240
Your brain literally makes up colors and white is one of those colors.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.900
So, are you ever going to look at color the same way again?

